🛠️ Ansible Modules: A Quick Reference Guide

Think of modules as the actual "tools" in Ansible's toolbox. Instead of writing complex bash scripts, you simply call the specific module designed to do the job for you.

They are categorized in two main ways: By Source (who builds them) and By Function (what they do).

1️⃣ Module Types by Source (The Architecture)

Historically, all tools were packed into Ansible. Today, they are separated into "Collections" so they can be updated independently.

🏛️ Core Modules (ansible.builtin)

What they are: The fundamental tools maintained directly by the core Ansible team.

Examples: copy, template, command, service.

How to get them: They ship with Ansible by default.

🌍 Community/Vendor Modules (community.* or vendor names)

What they are: Specialized tools maintained by partner vendors (like AWS or Cisco) or the open-source community.

Examples: kubernetes.core.k8s (for Pods) or community.docker.docker_image.

How to get them: Downloaded via Ansible Galaxy.

🧑‍💻 Custom Modules

What they are: Modules you write yourself (usually in Python) if a tool doesn't exist for your company's proprietary internal software.

